--
-- WHAT THIS PASS IS ANSWERING
-- F1 cleared the gov_regulation primary wall and left a different wall standing.
-- scripts/vr-federal-fpi.mjs --set all --why reported 911 formal-index rows whose
-- reason id was `vehicle_only` — the member met the issue ONLY inside a package, so
-- the engine refuses to name a direction — and 3,084 reading `incidental`, which is
-- the harder failure: four or more judged acts on the key and not one of them a
-- primary, so the wall in stance-helpers.js prints 'Not about this issue' about a key
-- the member has voted on repeatedly. Neither is curable by a heavier mapping, and
-- piling a fifth secondary on makes `incidental` worse rather than better. The only
-- cure is a standalone, contested, single-subject instrument in the chamber the
-- blocked member sits in. The 119th offers three and this migration lands all three.
--
-- · energy_production, the incidental hole. Six senators held four-plus secondary acts
--   and no primary. S.J.Res. 10 and S.J.Res. 71 are privileged National Emergencies Act
--   resolutions to terminate the Executive Order 14156 energy emergency — one operative
--   sentence each, no reserve fund, no severable provision, rejected 47-52 and 47-51 on
--   the Senate floor. Mapped w90 PRIMARY yea_opposes, because EO 14156 is already in
--   this record at w100 PRIMARY yea_supports and terminating it withdraws exactly those
--   authorities. energy_production incidental: 41 → 10.
-- · housing_build, the vehicle_only hole, and NO MAPPING IS ADDED FOR IT. H.R. 6644
--   already carries housing_build w100 PRIMARY and already has its House roll. What the
--   record was missing was the Senate half of the measure's own vote — senate 119/2 roll
--   53, 89-10. Attribution, not curation. housing_build vehicle_only: 121 → 26.
-- · america_first, the retraction. H.R. 1048's w70 row rests on countering Chinese
--   influence on U.S. campuses, and the chip's August 2026 scope note lists that ground
--   in its OUT column by name. F1 queued it as follow-up 0e. Removed by guarded DELETE.
--   The measure keeps its gov_transparency w100 PRIMARY, its edu_balance w55 and its
--   roll; what goes is one non-primary row on a key the vocabulary disowned.
--
-- THE COST, MEASURED AND NOT NETTED
-- A densification wave owes one promise: no row the engine already characterised stops
-- being characterised. Checked directly on the row model's `read` flag over all 327
-- federal pids — not on the shape counters, which can net out — this wave starts
-- characterising 112 rows (7 strong, 24 mostly, 81 thin) and STOPS characterising 29.
-- All 29 were the weakest tier and both causes are named in
-- db/vr-federal-mapping-seed-f2.json under readLossDisclosure: 27 senators whose only
-- permitting_reform act was a nay on H.R. 3746 now hold one act each way and read
-- `mixed_thin`, and 2 representatives whose america_first record was carried by the
-- retracted row. Neither is a primary wall trip. The first is the engine declining to
-- pretend a single vote was a characterisation once a contradicting vote arrives,
-- which is runbook rule 25 working as intended; refusing to attribute a real 89-10
-- Senate passage vote so that 27 one-vote rows keep looking read would be the
-- aggregate dictating the text. The Utah seven lose nothing.
--
-- KEYS LEFT BLOCKED, ON PURPOSE
-- · econ_workers — No admissible Senate instrument exists in the 119th. H.R. 5408, the
--   Faster Labor Contracts Act, is the key's PRIMARY and passed the House on roll
--   119/2/216 (230-193). It was received in the Senate 2026-06-10 and no vote has been
--   taken. The only Senate labor-and-pay instrument of the Congress is S. 3012, back pay
--   for excepted employees, and it never proceeded: cloture on the motion to proceed
--   failed twice, roll 119/1/585 (54-45) and roll 119/1/609 (53-43), both short of
--   sixty. Rule 8 admits passage, concurrence and conference reports; a failed cloture
--   vote on a motion to proceed is none of those. The minimum-wage vehicle, Sanders
--   Amdt. 2126 to H.Con.Res. 14 (roll 119/1/184, 47-52), is refused by rule 31 and was
--   already refused by F1 on the same ground. S. 129, No Tax on Tips, passed the Senate
--   by unanimous consent and has no roll call to attribute.
--   WOULD UNBLOCK IT: A Senate passage vote on H.R. 5408 or a sibling first-contract
--   bill. Nothing to do until then; piling a fifth secondary onto the key would deepen
--   the wall F1 documented rather than clear it.
-- · free_speech — No Senate instrument of any kind. All 890 roll calls in the Senate's
--   own vote menus for the 119th (vote_menu_119_1.xml and vote_menu_119_2.xml) were read
--   for subject. ZERO concern free speech, press freedom, platform moderation or campus
--   expression. The key's Senate-side instrument in the record is S. 146, the TAKE IT
--   DOWN Act, which carries free_speech w30 as a non-primary — and S. 146 passed the
--   Senate by UNANIMOUS CONSENT, so there is no roll call in existence to attribute, not
--   merely one we have not ingested.
--   WOULD UNBLOCK IT: A contested Senate vote on a speech or platform-moderation
--   measure. There is not one.
-- · health_rural — No PRIMARY instrument anywhere in the corpus, in either chamber,
--   which is why all 71 senators read vehicle_only. Every live health_rural row is a
--   provision inside a larger vehicle — the largest is the Consolidated Appropriations
--   Act, 2026 at weight 45. On the Senate side the menu sweep found only two shapes on
--   the subject: deficit-neutral reserve funds during the S.Con.Res. 7 vote-a-rama,
--   which rule 31 refuses by name and F1 already refused, and appropriations amendments
--   that are provisions by construction. A standalone rural-health bill did not reach a
--   Senate vote in the 119th.
--   WOULD UNBLOCK IT: A standalone rural-health or provider-payment bill with a floor
--   vote. Refusing to approximate one out of an appropriations amendment is the point of
--   the vehicle_only reason existing at all.
-- · scotus_reform — No PRIMARY instrument anywhere. 101 of 101 rows on the key read
--   vehicle_only. The menu sweep found the subject only in JUDICIAL NOMINATIONS, which
--   are confirmations of individuals and not measures on court structure; rule 8 does
--   not admit a nomination as an act on a policy key, and the record's PROCEDURAL_TYPES
--   / nomination handling keeps them out for the same reason. No term-limits,
--   court-expansion, jurisdiction-stripping or ethics-code bill reached a Senate vote in
--   the 119th.
--   WOULD UNBLOCK IT: A floor vote on court structure or a judicial ethics code. Note
--   this key is a candidate for the deeper question of whether a key with no primary in
--   either chamber should be publishing rows at all — raised here, not answered.
-- · econ_smallbiz — No Senate instrument. The menu sweep found the subject only in SBA
--   nominations. Same refusal as scotus_reform: a confirmation is not an act on the
--   policy.
--   WOULD UNBLOCK IT: A contested Senate vote on a small-business bill.
--
-- CANDIDATE ROLL CALLS READ AND DECLINED
-- · S.J.Res. 80 (senate 119/1/599, 52-45 Passed) — REFUSED AS A MAPPING, not as a vote.
--   This is the CRA disapproval of the NPR-A leasing rule, and it is the most tempting
--   candidate this wave rejected: it is standalone, single-subject, Senate-originated,
--   decisively contested and about energy. Rule 3 decides it — 'gov_regulation is for
--   measures whose primary operative purpose is regulation itself, explicitly CRA
--   disapprovals' — so its primary axis is gov_regulation, and the corpus is already
--   consistent about where energy sits on a leasing CRA: H.J.Res. 131, the enacted
--   BLM-leasing CRA, files energy_production as a SECONDARY at weight 75, non-primary.
--   Admitting S.J.Res. 80 the same way would add the FIFTH non-primary energy_production
--   row — precisely the 'fifth secondary' rule 30 forbids and the mechanism that
--   produced F1's adverse finding, where a fourth non-primary act pushes a member across
--   _RD_MIN_JUDGED onto the deepEnough branch and the primary wall then reads the row as
--   `incidental`. It would make the key measurably WORSE. Filing it as a primary instead
--   would contradict rule 3 and every CRA already in the record. Left out.
-- · S.J.Res. 80 (senate 119/1/595, 54-46 Agreed to) — Motion to proceed. Not a decisive
--   question under rule 8, and moot given the mapping refusal above.
-- · H.R. 6644 (senate 119/2/182, 85-5 Agreed to) — Motion to concur. Refused twice over:
--   'On the Motion' is not a passage form under rule 12, and at 5.556% of the yea+nay
--   pool the losing side is below the one-tenth bar, so the vote differentiates nobody.
--   This is the later act on the measure and it is still refused — recency does not beat
--   the near-unanimity floor.
-- · H.R. 6644 (senate 119/2/51, 84-10 Agreed to) — Scott (SC) Amdt. 4308, a substitute
--   in the nature of. Rule 8 allows one decisive act per chamber per measure and roll 53
--   is it — the vote on the bill as amended, which is the text the Senate actually sent
--   back. Admitting the substitute as well would double-count one Senate decision.
-- · H.R. 6644 (senate 119/2/175 and 119/2/45, 87-8 and 90-8) — Motions to proceed, and
--   not decisive questions under rule 8, which admits passage, concurrence and
--   conference only. A motion to proceed asks whether the Senate will take the bill up
--   at all; it is answered before the chamber has voted on the policy, and 87-8 and 90-8
--   are what agreeing to debate looks like rather than what agreeing to the bill looks
--   like. Both are also below rule 11's one-tenth bar (8.421% and 8.163%), so even if
--   the question form were admissible the margin would not be. The Senate's one decisive
--   act on this measure is roll 53, and rule 8 allows exactly one per chamber per
--   measure.
-- · H.R. 6644 (senate 119/2/180, 119/2/52, 119/2/50, 119/2/44, 84-8, 82-11, 89-9, 84-6)
--   — Cloture motions. Not decisive questions under rule 8, and all four are below the
--   one-tenth bar besides.
-- · S. 3012 (senate 119/1/585 and 119/1/609, 54-45 and 53-43, both failed) — Cloture on
--   the motion to proceed, twice, both short of sixty. The bill never proceeded, so
--   there is no act on it. This is the whole of econ_workers' Senate-side story.
-- · Sanders Amdt. 2126 to H.Con.Res. 14 (senate 119/1/184, 47-52) — Rule 31. A
--   budget-resolution amendment of this class is a reserve-fund or point-of-order device
--   rather than an act on the underlying policy; F1 refused it on the same ground and
--   nothing has changed.
-- · S. 1582 (senate 119/1/318, 68-30 Passed) — Not refused on the merits — simply not on
--   any key this wave targets, and not read for mapping. Recorded here so the sweep's
--   coverage claim is checkable: the sweep looked at every passage-form roll in the
--   119th, and this is one it passed over deliberately rather than missed.
-- · S. 331 (senate 119/1/127, 84-16 Passed) — Already in the record with its mapping.
--   Listed so a reader can see the sweep found it and did not need to act.
--
-- H.R. 6644 CARRIES NO NEW MAPPING FROM THIS PASS, and that is the decision. PURE
-- ATTRIBUTION. No mapping is added, changed or removed. The measure's four live rows
-- (housing_build w100 PRIMARY, housing w80, permitting_reform w60, crypto_cbdc w40)
-- are all correct and all stay exactly as an earlier pass wrote them, rationales
-- included. What this wave gives the measure is the missing half of its own vote: the
-- Senate passed the standalone bill 89-10 and the record held only the House
-- concurrence. The migration pins the row count at 4 so a later pass cannot re-stuff
-- the measure while landing an attribution.
